  <p class="crumbs"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/guides">Guides</a> › How to learn Italian: a beginner plan</p>
  <h1>How to learn Italian: a beginner plan</h1>
  <p class="lede">Italian is one of the most musical and beginner-friendly languages: its pronunciation is very regular, its spelling matches its sounds, and thousands of words are familiar from English, food and music. Start with sounds and verbs, then speak from the first week.</p>

  <h2>Step 1 — Learn Italian pronunciation</h2>
  <p>Italian has seven vowel sounds and very regular spelling — once you learn the sounds, you can read any Italian word. Learn the double consonants early: “anno” (year) and “ano” (anus) differ only in the double “n”, so pronunciation really matters. Also learn “c” and “g”: before e and i they are soft (like “ch” and “j”), before a, o and u they are hard.</p>
  <p>Read Italian aloud every day. Italian is a language made for speaking out loud, and reading aloud is the fastest way to train your mouth.</p>
  <h2>Step 2 — Learn gender and articles</h2>
  <p>Every Italian noun is masculine or feminine. Most nouns ending in -o are masculine (il libro) and most ending in -a are feminine (la casa). Learn each noun with its article — il, lo, la — so the gender becomes part of the word. Words ending in -e can be either, so those you simply have to learn.</p>
  <p>Start with useful words: greetings (ciao, buongiorno, grazie, per favore), numbers, time, food, family and everyday verbs like essere (to be), avere (to have) and andare (to go).</p>
  <h2>Step 3 — Learn the present tense</h2>
  <p>Italian verbs fall into three groups: -are (parlare), -ere (vedere) and -ire (dormire). Learn the endings for -are verbs first — io parlo, tu parli, lui parla — then the other groups. Add the irregular verbs essere, avere and andare early, because you will use them in almost every sentence.</p>
  <p>As in other Romance languages, you do not need every tense at once. The present tense plus time words (oggi, domani, ieri) lets you talk about your day, your plans and your past.</p>
  <h2>Step 4 — Listen and read every day</h2>
  <p>Italian music, films and food culture give you endless listening material. Listen to Italian songs and learn the lyrics — songs are one of the best ways to learn Italian, because the words are clear and repetitive. Read short texts: menus, travel guides, children’s stories.</p>
  <p>When you read, notice how questions are formed and how Italians use gestures of language — words like “allora”, “dunque” and “ecco” that fill real conversation.</p>
  <h2>Step 5 — Speak, with feeling</h2>
  <p>Italians are famously encouraging to learners, so speak from week one: introduce yourself, order food, ask simple questions. Do not worry about sounding perfect — enthusiasm and a smile go a long way in Italian.</p>
  <p>A tutor helps you practise the conversations you actually need, and corrects the small mistakes — like double consonants and verb endings — that make the difference between being understood and sounding natural.</p>
  <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Ignoring double consonants — in Italian they change the meaning of words.</li>
    <li>Learning nouns without il or la, then guessing the gender.</li>
    <li>Translating English phrases word for word.</li>
    <li>Only studying grammar and never speaking.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Practical tips</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>Read aloud every day — Italian spelling makes this very rewarding.</li>
    <li>Learn verbs with their person: io parlo, tu parli — not just the infinitive.</li>
    <li>Learn one Italian song a week; the words will stick.</li>
    <li>Learn nouns with their article from the start.</li>
  </ul>
