  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>

  <details><summary>How long does it take to learn Italian?</summary><p>Italian is one of the quicker languages for English speakers. With 30 minutes a day, most learners can hold simple conversations in 3–6 months and reach a comfortable everyday level in 1–2 years. Regular pronunciation and familiar vocabulary make the start easy.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Is Italian hard to learn?</summary><p>The beginning is easy: regular sounds and spelling. The middle gets harder: verb endings, gender and the subjunctive mood take time. But Italian grammar is regular and logical, and there is wonderful music, food and film to practise with.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Do I need to learn the subjunctive?</summary><p>Not as a beginner. The subjunctive is used for wishes, doubts and opinions, and you will meet it naturally as you advance. Learn the present, past and future first — those cover most everyday conversation.</p></details>
  <details><summary>Can I learn Italian by myself?</summary><p>Yes, for vocabulary, grammar, listening and reading. Speaking needs a real person to talk to and correct you — that is the part a tutor is for.</p></details>
